2-93e1-5f9f-b4e8-6d22449e2db3" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186670804 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186670808 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1077 "${y}^{′}+4xy=2y$" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1078 #edb/ref 17592186670808 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670843 1079 #uuid "df3928d3-9553-5b66-b88b-336095f3c563" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670844 1080 "true" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670844 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670844 1083 #uuid "e7142a29-9310-5bc9-9b7b-532824bb0e33" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670806 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670819 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670838 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1072 #uuid "ea086a0d-6281-50e8-9859-feb937690d11" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670845 1074 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=-4x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-4x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=2y$; ${y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=y$ only." 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670846 1080 "false" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670846 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670846 1083 #uuid "f9763a24-d037-571b-9f03-46dc3f319384" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670803 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670807 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186670839 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1072 #uuid "fa221a93-5c26-51e7-9d03-310306b15d77" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670847 1074 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $-5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y & =3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y-3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{2}-3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-4y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y & =-4y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{2}+4)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=3y$; ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-4y$ only." 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670848 1168 "generators/89/89da60eff9352611289e3b33adaef5c15c4a340472ea492d7e6427c578d5713d/17592186087868.py" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true] [17592186670848 1169 #uuid "9ce90597-a632-53d0-a825-e919fcd5b202" 13194139538587 17592186045443 true]] :edb/tempids {"062d8748-bc07-5aa6-9588-2b82d547067c" 17592186670798 "09311cc0-a9e1-5e64-bf4b-40e0a4d78186" 17592186670799 "09e9dea5-b7d0-57c3-9af6-d37ced5b1167" 17592186670800 "0f60f433-4104-544d-ba20-53ce8d39c651" 17592186670801 "16487eca-6999-56b0-bb66-0b337dab9a14" 17592186670802 "17a8d885-5fc2-5eaf-9a04-ecea73a2524b" 17592186670803 "1a45a63b-566f-5c4c-940b-0a8f8df66a60" 17592186670804 "1d186eeb-24d9-5746-81b4-03bdfbb3946c" 17592186670805 "297a4864-aebf-59c7-846e-7447703e47f1" 17592186670806 "2bff5616-0cc4-5b8a-938e-76f98cb89377" 17592186670807 "2d6fd131-4d4b-5c1d-87bc-fd9d9eee9504" 17592186670808 "32e86b1c-ca7c-583b-acda-148bc8829f40" 17592186670809 "33b0716a-3a08-565d-ab4d-7ef439c1f8bd" 17592186670810 "362509b0-7f13-5268-9882-b7458181febd" 17592186670811 "3eea5aa8-c67d-5ad3-911c-0f9b71c1f33e" 17592186670812 "4a514407-d3f3-575f-b9fa-d8b32140637f" 17592186670813 "552f